    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Clients can now have their own payment terms in days. For a client with terms, an invoice is due that many days after its issue date, whether it is created, sent or duplicated. `bin/e2e` passes all 3 tests in the spec, and the replay of earlier test data ran without complaint. Duplicating isn't covered by those tests, but it uses the same due-date logic as creating and sending.\n\n**What changed:**\n- **Stored data:** a new migration, `V12__client_terms.sql`, adds an optional `terms_days` column to clients. Empty means the old rule still applies: due at the end of the following month.\n- **Server:** clients now carry `termsDays`, and adding a client accepts it. Creating, sending and duplicating an invoice all go through one helper in `InvoiceService` that applies the client's terms when they have them.\n- **Test endpoints:** the seed endpoint now stores `clients[].termsDays` and refuses a value that isn't a whole number of 0 or more. The fixture read-back returns it.\n- **Front end:** the add-client form has an optional \"Payment terms in days\" field (`data-testid=\"client-form-terms\"`), and the client data now includes `termsDays`.\n\n**A limit I chose:** when you add a client, terms must be a whole number from 1 to 365, and the form and server both reject anything else. The request didn't give a range, so tell me if you want different limits. The test seed accepts any whole number of 0 or more, so it stores whatever a fixture gives it.\n\nYou can only set terms when adding a client. There's no way yet to change them on an existing client, since the request didn't ask for that.",
